WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:08.700
Bodies all over the ground, necks twisted,&nbsp;
faces contorted in terror. SCP-173 - not one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:15.120
but an army - scatters out through Site-19,&nbsp;
massacring researchers one broken neck at a time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:21.660
Pure chaos. How could this possibly have happened?
The strobing of the blood-red emergency lighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.280
was already fear-inducing enough. So was being&nbsp;
hidden under one of the tables in the mess hall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:31.140
trembling as the noise of alarms rang out&nbsp;
through the facility. But the thing about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.520
flickering lights scared SCP Foundation&nbsp;
Researcher Mira Smythe all the more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.800
were the few seconds of darkness that&nbsp;
came between each flash of red. If it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.000
had been any other containment breach,&nbsp;
she probably wouldn’t have even noticed;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.200
for in those few seconds, when the&nbsp;
warning light lit up only to cut out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:55.140
she couldn’t see a thing. And her not being able&nbsp;
to see made it easier for them to move around.&nbsp;

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:00.540
Mira had lost track of time since the alarms had&nbsp;
first sounded to mark the containment breach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:05.160
She’d tried to keep count of the minutes, but&nbsp;
the drumming of her heartbeat had thrown her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:10.200
off. There was no way of knowing if it had been&nbsp;
hours, or only a few minutes since everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.360
went wrong. Of course, the SCP Foundation&nbsp;
had plenty of procedures and protocols in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.520
place to prevent or respond to situations&nbsp;
like this. Yet, the emergency alarms kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.560
echoing through the corridors, lights plunging&nbsp;
the facility into darkness every few seconds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.080
only to break it up with a brief flash of&nbsp;
red. It seemed to Mira, from her hiding spot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:37.020
that this breach had proven far too much the&nbsp;
Foundation’s extensive procedures to counteract.&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.760
Maybe it was all over already, she thought&nbsp;
after a few more seconds, or maybe minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:48.000
under the table. What if… they had gotten bored,&nbsp;
tired of slaughtering their way through Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.040
personnel and slunk back to their containment&nbsp;
chamber. That would’ve been simpler, far less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.260
terrifying than the alternative, that came&nbsp;
creeping into Mira’s head in the form of her next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:04.140
thought. What if she was the only one left alive,&nbsp;
the last member of site personnel still breathing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:10.860
just by some random a hundred to one chance?&nbsp;
And if that was the case, how long would it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:18.000
take them to find her? How many even were there?
A new sound, just detectable below the alarms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.240
broke through her avalanche of anxious questions.&nbsp;
Footsteps, the noise of boots clomping against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.640
the floor, clearly belonging to a human and&nbsp;
not one of them. Cautious as to which way her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:35.280
gaze was directed, Mira poked her head out from&nbsp;
beneath the table, glancing around during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.140
few seconds where the red of the emergency lights&nbsp;
allowed her to see. The steps were getting closer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:45.060
and as they did, Researcher Smythe realized&nbsp;
something that made her fearful for her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:50.280
fellow survivor. They were running away, and&nbsp;
there was no way they could do that while also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.380
looking back at what was coming after them…
“Over here!” she hissed in a hushed tone at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.720
the sight of a pair of legs&nbsp;
crossing a nearby corridor.&nbsp;

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:02.580
Their thick, standard-issue military boots seemed&nbsp;
to match those worn by the Foundation’s security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:08.040
personnel. The officer passed by, only to stop&nbsp;
and tiptoe closer to the mess hall, having heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.600
Mira’s whispered call. With the red lights&nbsp;
blinking, it looked like every other step the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.820
security officer took had been edited out, like&nbsp;
they were teleporting closer in the dark. Watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.860
intently from her hiding place, it reminded Mira&nbsp;
sickeningly of the things she was hiding from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:28.860
how they moved… and the worrying fact that she&nbsp;
couldn’t see one following the security officer.&nbsp;

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:32.640
“Hello?” called a man’s voice&nbsp;
as he entered the mess hall.&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.620
“Under the table,” Mira whispered. “If we hide,&nbsp;
we can still see them, but they can’t see us!”&nbsp;

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.480
“Hiding?” he replied. “No, we need something that&nbsp;
can revert these things back to the original. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.800
was so much easier to handle when there was just&nbsp;
one of them. Look, let’s make a break for it; I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.900
gonna grab SCP-3108 and then get the lights back&nbsp;
on. If we can restore visibility, we might be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:56.460
to stand a fighting chance against these things.”
The officer was looking around the cafeteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:01.260
trying to locate the source of Researcher&nbsp;
Smythe’s voice, eyes distracted. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:05.340
the intermittent flickering of red light&nbsp;
cut off again, plunging the entire mess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:10.140
hall into near-pitch darkness again. At the&nbsp;
same time, there was the sound of something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:16.020
heavy and stone scraping against the flooring.
It turned Mira’s stomach, and she immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:21.660
covered her mouth. She had no idea if they could&nbsp;
hear her breathing. Could a creature of stone even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:26.700
detect sound at all? While the lights were down&nbsp;
and the security guard's back was turned, a pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:31.800
of sculptures, built from concrete and rebar,&nbsp;
traces of spray paint over their stone skin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:37.740
appeared behind the officer at impossible speed.
There was a sickening crunch, followed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.400
sound of something dropping to the floor&nbsp;
of the mess hall. As the emergency lights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:47.700
flicked back on, Mira could see them, the two&nbsp;
copies of SCP-173, standing still next to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.680
table she was under, bathed in red light.
This nightmare had all begun as an otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:59.040
ordinary Friday for the SCP Foundation. Only a&nbsp;
few hours earlier, Mira had been brainstorming her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.860
latest pitch for a series of experiments involving&nbsp;
SCP-914. Otherwise known as the Clockworks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:12.840
SCP-914 was a large machine comprised of various&nbsp;
cogs, gyros, and gears, of unknown origin. Its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:17.520
function, however, had long been documented&nbsp;
and was of great interest to the Foundation.&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:24.120
Any object placed within SCP-914’s Input booth,&nbsp;
could be transformed, destroyed, or improved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:29.460
depending on the Clockworks’ setting. ‘Rough’&nbsp;
would disintegrate a subject, ‘Coarse’ would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:34.920
dismantle it to its base components, and ‘1:1’&nbsp;
would merely replace the objected with a similar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:40.680
almost identical one. Then, there were the ‘Fine’&nbsp;
and ‘Very Fine’ settings. The former would cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:47.160
SCP-914 to improve any item, and the latter is&nbsp;
capable of the same but with one key difference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:53.100
‘Very Fine’ would refine an object even more,&nbsp;
usually by giving it anomalous properties.&nbsp;

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:58.320
The Foundation higher-ups had permitted staff&nbsp;
to freely conduct tests using the Clockworks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:04.140
although approval was required and all data was&nbsp;
to be recorded. While many of the scientists at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:09.840
the Foundation were fixed on the mysterious&nbsp;
origin of SCP-914, or the furthest extent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.460
its refinement capabilities, Mira had been&nbsp;
thinking about it in a far more practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:21.540
way. She wasn’t interested in the how or why&nbsp;
of SCP-914’s inner workings, nor who had built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:26.880
it. Her experiments were born out of considering&nbsp;
better usages for the Clockworks. It was a machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:32.760
that could provide endless possibilities, and Mira&nbsp;
was determined to make to the fullest use of it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:37.440
order to solve actual real-world problems.
“Imagine, for a moment,” she announced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:41.100
reading off her cue cards as she practiced&nbsp;
pitching her experiment to the Foundation’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:45.960
Senior Researchers. “Imagine a world where&nbsp;
nobody ever gets sick, where human beings are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:52.320
able to live long, healthy and prosperous lives.”
“Maybe dial back on the Star Trek,” her colleague&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.220
Research Assistant Jensen interjected.
“Really, that’s what you call constructive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.220 --> 00:07:01.560
criticism?” she retorted, before continuing. “If&nbsp;
we apply the transformative, refinement power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:08.040
of SCP-914, that world could be the future&nbsp;
that awaits us. For example, I’m proposing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:12.900
tests that involve placing man-made chemical&nbsp;
compounds like painkillers, heart medication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:17.040
and other potentially life-saving medicines&nbsp;
through the Clockworks’ refinement process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.840
I’m not suggesting we need to set it to ‘Very&nbsp;
Fine’ and create anomalous versions of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.180
things. But what if, even on the ‘Fine’ setting,&nbsp;
we’re able to improve the effectiveness of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:32.520
existing treatments for all kinds of afflictions?”
Jensen raised their hand, patiently waiting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.160
comment like a child in a classroom.
“What?”&nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:40.740
“A few things,” they replied, lowering their&nbsp;
hand. “First off, some if not all ‘man-made’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:45.210
medications include some kind of organic&nbsp;
component. The O5 Council have banned all –”&nbsp;

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:50.220
“Yes, I am fully aware of the Containment&nbsp;
Procedures,” Mira interrupted them. “That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.400
exactly what I’m trying to convince the&nbsp;
Council, or hell even just Doctor Bright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.660
to let me step around. I genuinely&nbsp;
believe we could be doing so much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:03.600
with SCP-914, and I need them to see that.”
“Look, I’m not here to rain on your parade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.340
Smythe,” her assistant reassured. “I’m only&nbsp;
bringing up concerns that anyone will have about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:13.200
this proposal. You talked about wanting to improve&nbsp;
our existing medical treatments, which is noble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:17.580
don’t get me wrong. But have you considered&nbsp;
the wider effect that might have? If you make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:22.860
everyone on Earth more resistant or even immune&nbsp;
to everything, then diseases are going to evolve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:28.140
What if we end up with a form of super bacteria,&nbsp;
resistant to all forms of medication, even if that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:33.360
medicine’s been refined in SCP-914? Trying to&nbsp;
save the world might just bring about its end.”&nbsp;

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:37.680
“I’ve thought about that, but if the Council&nbsp;
starts allowing for biological testing again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:43.080
then the solution is obvious: we use the&nbsp;
Clockworks to eradicate whatever germs or diseases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:48.120
are able to develop after improving treatment&nbsp;
options,” Mira explained, then adding: “Besides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:53.340
that can’t be any more dangerous than what we’ve&nbsp;
been using SCP-914 for. I mean, enhancing other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:58.740
SCPs? That’s a recipe for disaster!”
“Got to agree with you there,” Jensen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:01.920
sighed. “Even without placing them in&nbsp;
the Clockworks, the Foundation’s been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:05.580
way too cavalier with the dangerous ones.&nbsp;
Speaking of, you know what tonight is?”&nbsp;

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:09.120
Smythe replied with a confused look.
“Date night,” they stated.&nbsp;

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:14.640
Elsewhere in the facility, unknown to Researcher&nbsp;
Smythe at that time, one of the dangerous SCPs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.320
Jenson had mentioned had been let out of&nbsp;
containment. This was a regular occurrence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:25.080
even if it was considered a little unorthodox to&nbsp;
some. In fact, the only reason that the O5 Council&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:31.440
so often turned a blind eye to it was because of&nbsp;
the researcher it involved: Doctor Alto Clef. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:37.260
seemed Clef had a certain fondness for SCP-173,&nbsp;
the Sculpture: a living statue constructed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:42.540
from concrete and rebar that could travel at&nbsp;
impossible speeds whenever it was unobserved.&nbsp;

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:48.360
Most Friday nights, Clef would have romantic&nbsp;
candlelit dinners with SCP-173, putting a dress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:52.800
and blonde wig on the Sculpture and treating&nbsp;
it like his beloved partner. The whole while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:56.280
watched by Doctor Clef and a group of&nbsp;
D Class acting as waiters and cooks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:02.160
SCP-173 would stand motionless while the&nbsp;
bizarre date went on late into the night.&nbsp;

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:05.520
It was no secret that the Sculpture&nbsp;
was a highly aggressive creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:09.600
with a penchant for snapping the neck of the&nbsp;
nearest person the moment that they blinked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.220
However, it was only because of Doctor Clef’s&nbsp;
reputation and track record for the elimination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:20.160
of anomalies that he was granted the special&nbsp;
privilege of wining and dining with SCP-173.&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:24.900
This time, though, part way through their&nbsp;
regularly scheduled date, Doctor Clef was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:28.800
called away to deal with matters relating&nbsp;
to another anomaly he’d been charged with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:33.900
‘decommissioning’. The pair of D Class waiting on&nbsp;
the doctor and his date were instructed to wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:39.480
keeping a close eye on SCP-173, alerting&nbsp;
each other in case they needed to blink.&nbsp;

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:44.760
It was a hell of a gamble, a risk that could have&nbsp;
been avoided if the Sculpture had been transported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:50.520
back to its secure containment chamber. But Doctor&nbsp;
Clef could be ‘persuasive’… that is to say, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:55.440
threatened both D Class waiters with a bludgeoning&nbsp;
from his ukulele if they didn’t do as he asked.&nbsp;

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:01.140
Both the D Class stared directly at the Sculpture,&nbsp;
each one feeling the anxious beads of sweat slowly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:05.580
crawling down their foreheads, worried that&nbsp;
they’d both blink at the same time by accident.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:10.560
They announced their need to refresh their eyes&nbsp;
every so often, alternating between being the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:16.920
watcher and the one allowed to blink. All the&nbsp;
while, SCP-173 was motionless. Perhaps it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:22.020
patiently waiting for Doctor Clef to return… or&nbsp;
for one of the D Class watching it to slip up.&nbsp;

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:26.160
As if through some cruel twist of fate,&nbsp;
a fly began to buzz through the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:31.620
drifting towards the D Class with their eyes&nbsp;
on SCP-173. It looped and darted around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:36.600
its wings making an annoying droning. One of&nbsp;
the waiters announced he needed to blink again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:40.500
and the other told him he was free to do&nbsp;
so while he kept watch over the Sculpture.&nbsp;

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:46.260
At the exact same moment that one man blinked,&nbsp;
the fly zipped its way towards the second D Class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:51.660
distracting him and drawing his gaze for little&nbsp;
more than a millisecond. That fly whizzing past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:58.080
was the last thing he saw, as SCP-173 darted&nbsp;
forward. It moved with such incredible speed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:03.840
it was imperceptible, grabbing the nearby D Class&nbsp;
– the one who’d spotted the fly – and killing him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:10.140
instantly before the other could even open his&nbsp;
eyes. When he did, SCP-173 had vanished from its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:15.240
spot at the date night table, nowhere to be seen.
Before the remaining D Class could realize it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:19.920
was behind him, SCP-173 had made&nbsp;
short work of its other observer.&nbsp;

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:25.620
It began zipping unseen through the corridors&nbsp;
of the SCP Foundation facility. Doctor Clef had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:31.800
returned to his date only to find the two D Class&nbsp;
waiters dead, and SCP-173 missing. Immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:36.900
he had raised the alarm, meaning that research&nbsp;
staff were too busy fleeing to safety to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:41.760
any attention to the Sculpture quickly making its&nbsp;
way through the site. Swept up in the evacuation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:47.460
half-deafened by alarms, in all the commotion,&nbsp;
Researcher Mira Smythe hadn’t even realized she’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:51.840
left the door to the Clockworks wide open.
Shuffling down a corridor with her fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:56.400
researchers, the lights suddenly went&nbsp;
out. A few nearby screamed in panic at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:01.800
having been unceremoniously thrust into the&nbsp;
dark. Mira blindly reached for Jensen’s hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:07.380
only to hear the sounds of multiple heavy&nbsp;
concrete feet dragging along the floor. Suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:14.160
the emergency lights blinked on. She turned&nbsp;
and, to her horror, saw SCP-173 with its stony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:19.860
arms around Jensen’s neck, their face stricken&nbsp;
with sheer terror, tears in their eyes. But now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:24.600
Mira quickly realized, as the corridor was filled&nbsp;
with shrieks of fear from the other researchers…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:29.520
that there was more than one Sculpture now.
Mira barely managed to escape with her life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:34.140
in all the confusion, crawling out of the&nbsp;
low-lit corridor on her hands and knees while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:40.200
the multiple new SCP-173 instances slaughtered&nbsp;
anyone that couldn’t see them. Since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:44.880
she had been hiding, hearing the scraping steps&nbsp;
of the Sculptures traveling around the facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:50.520
unobserved. They had attacked the fuse boxes&nbsp;
first, as if in a calculated tactical move:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:55.200
take out the lights, and suddenly&nbsp;
everyone found it much harder to see them.&nbsp;

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:59.940
Even through the fear, it was hard for Mira&nbsp;
not to blame herself for all the chaos. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:04.980
couldn’t remember whether she’d left the door&nbsp;
to SCP-914 open or not, but it hadn’t taken her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:09.480
long to figure out that the Clockworks had&nbsp;
something to do with his madness. Somehow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:15.060
SCP-173 had made its way there, had been able&nbsp;
to operate the machinery while the Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:21.900
staff were distracted by their own evacuation.&nbsp;
Presumably, with SCP-914 set to ‘Very Fine’, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:28.920
Sculpture had been transformed into its ultimate,&nbsp;
refined form… or multiple forms, it seemed.&nbsp;

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:34.500
There was a veritable horde of SCP-173s running&nbsp;
amok through the facility. It made sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:40.140
Mira reasoned, in some twisted, cruel anomalous&nbsp;
way. The Sculpture had only ever been a danger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:44.760
when unobserved; the second anyone took&nbsp;
their eyes off it, so much as blinked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:51.360
it would strike. How does a machine like SCP-914&nbsp;
improve upon that? Easy, it gives the Sculpture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:57.180
the power to replicate itself. Anyone can look at&nbsp;
multiple things at once, of course, but nobody can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.180 --> 00:15:04.500
look at everything simultaneously. Even if someone&nbsp;
was watching a pair, or three SCP-173s, one more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:09.600
could easily slip up behind them for the kill…
Still hidden under the table, Mira’s eyes were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:14.580
locked on the pair of Sculptures in the mess hall,&nbsp;
the body of the security officer out of focus in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:20.460
her peripheral vision. She dared not look away,&nbsp;
frozen in sheer fear that the second she blinked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:25.380
they would lift up the table and find her. She&nbsp;
had been so overwhelmed, so paralyzed with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:30.540
terror that she had forgotten about the lights…
As the emergency lighting flickered again, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:36.060
Sculptures both moved, shifting across the mess&nbsp;
hall as the room when dark, only to stop in place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:41.280
the second Mira could see them again. They hadn’t&nbsp;
gotten closer. In fact, both looked to be leaving.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:48.240
The only thing keeping them here was… her. She was&nbsp;
watching them, and it had frozen them in place.&nbsp;

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:54.480
Their greatest strength, the imperceptible&nbsp;
movement speed of SCP-173, was offset by an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.480 --> 00:16:00.060
indisputable weakness of their anomalous biology;&nbsp;
they couldn’t stop someone from looking at them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:06.060
and couldn’t stop themselves becoming still,&nbsp;
solid concrete when they were being watched.&nbsp;

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:10.500
Mulling over her options, Mira decided to follow&nbsp;
through with the plan from the fallen security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:17.640
guard, to retrieve SCP-3108 and try to undo what&nbsp;
the Clockworks had done. Slowly, she slipped out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:22.920
from under the table, keeping both eyes on the&nbsp;
pair of Sculptures, keeping them lashed to one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:28.320
spot as she gradually backed out of the mess hall.
The lights went out and plunged the area into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:33.060
darkness again, and Researcher Smythe could&nbsp;
hear the heavy, concrete scraping of the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:37.920
Sculptures looking for her as she blindly made&nbsp;
her way through the facility. She managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:43.920
evade the roaming stone creatures, resisting her&nbsp;
natural instinct to hide and looking directly at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:49.260
any she encountered on the way, making sure to&nbsp;
stay out of their view. If she saw them, she’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:56.100
freeze them. If they saw her, they’d chase her.
Eventually, fishing SCP-3108 out of a containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.100 --> 00:17:01.740
box, Mira held the ‘weapon’ in her hand. It was&nbsp;
a heavily modified Nerf gun, which she loaded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:07.260
a foam dart into and pulled the plastic slide&nbsp;
back. The creatively named ‘Nerfing Gun’ had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:14.280
almost the opposite effect to SCP-914; instead of&nbsp;
improving something, SCP-3108 turned whatever was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:20.700
shot with it into an inferior equivalent object,&nbsp;
something worse than itself. It might just work.&nbsp;

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:25.080
Drawing the gun as she heard the sound of&nbsp;
scraping concrete getting nearer and nearer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:30.000
Mira turned the corner, coming face to face with&nbsp;
one of the Sculptures. She held the gun high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:35.040
closing the gap between them, making sure she&nbsp;
didn’t miss but wary she needed to fire before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:39.420
the lights blinked off again. She squeezed the&nbsp;
plastic trigger, sending the foam dart shooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:44.640
out of the barrel of the Nerfing Gun. The flimsy&nbsp;
projectile collided with the SCP-173 instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:48.960
bouncing off its head and landing on the floor.
For a split second, the lights dimmed again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:54.120
then came back on. Mira’s heart had lurched&nbsp;
in her chest, only to see that the Sculpture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:59.400
hadn’t moved at all. She leaned closer to it,&nbsp;
blinking intentionally. It still didn’t move.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:06.300
It was rooted to the ground, fixed. Just a statue,&nbsp;
rendered powerless by the Nerfing Gun. Excitedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:09.540
Mira knelt down and scrabbled around on&nbsp;
the floor to retrieve the dart, just as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:13.680
the emergency lighting flickered out again.
In her excitement, Mira was too focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:19.920
reloading SCP-3108. That is, until she heard the&nbsp;
shuffling of concrete and rebar behind her. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:24.780
lights were still out, and she fumbled blindly for&nbsp;
the foam dart, as the noise got closer and closer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:29.280
Her fingertips brushed the projectile, knocking&nbsp;
it further away, when suddenly the lights came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:34.380
back up. But as she reached for it, Researcher&nbsp;
Smythe felt a large, cold presence behind her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:39.660
something huge and heavy… and made of stone,&nbsp;
lingering at the very corner of her eye.&nbsp;

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:44.640
Before she could turn around and face it,&nbsp;
something grabbed Mira, and a sickening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:50.760
snap rang out through the corridor…
Now go check out “SCP-096 VS. SCP-173”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:57.600
and “SCP-173 ORIGIN STORY - HOW 173 GOT&nbsp;
TO SITE-19”? Just be careful not to blink…

